# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 topology
# TCP three-way handshake + data on the third segment (RFC 9293 §3.5).
# Two endpoints, three directed segments carrying the connection-setup
# exchange. Each segment's ordinal (1/2/3) is in its edge label.
#
# HONEST LIMIT (guide/expressing.md known limits — "strict message ordering"):
# message ORDER is carried only by the 1/2/3 numbering convention in the
# labels, a naming convention that `MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE` does not treat as first-class order.
# The sequence genre (`MESSAGE-ORDER-AND-STATE`, closed 0.4) makes order first-class;
# this figure stays figdown 0.1 DELIBERATELY, so any released engine can
# render it, and a reader here must trust the ordinal prefixes below.
# Likewise, the canonical RFC 9293 figure carries the per-endpoint TCP
# STATE (LISTEN, SYN-SENT, …) on the lifelines; the topology genre has no
# lifeline-state construct, so the companion `table` block below carries
# those states machine-readably — composition (multiple typed blocks in one
# document, in document order) is the ruled interim until sequence lands.
title "TCP Three-Way Handshake"

# Semantic classes: the label states the MEANING; colour merely renders it,
# and the legend derives automatically (spec §2.7). Each segment is classed
# by what it does — a reader queries the class, not the arrow colour.
class setup "Connection-setup segment — carries SYN and/or ACK control flags" stroke=#2563eb
class data  "Segment that also carries application data (piggybacked on the ACK)" stroke=#16a34a

node client "Client (active open)"
node server "Server (passive open, LISTEN)"

# left-to-right reading: client on the left issues the active open.
flow right

# Segment 1: client -> server, SYN, seq=x (flags: SYN)
edge client -[1: SYN  seq=x]-> server class=setup

# Segment 2: server -> client, SYN+ACK, seq=y ack=x+1 (flags: SYN, ACK)
edge server -[2: SYN,ACK  seq=y ack=x+1]-> client class=setup

# Segment 3: client -> server, ACK ack=y+1, carrying piggybacked data
# (flags: ACK). One segment, so ONE edge; it is both an ACK and a data
# carrier, so it is classed as data (the stronger claim). Detail in prose.
edge client -[3: ACK  ack=y+1  (+ data)]-> server class=data

# Hybrid panels: a second figdown section below (multi-section `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`),
# not nested under the scene header. Each section has one genre (`GENRE-KEYWORD-ALLOWLIST`).

# --- panel: separate genre section (main-standard hybrid = multi-section `MULTI-FIGURE-DOCUMENTS`) ---
figdown 0.1 table
table state "Endpoint state after each segment (RFC 9293 §3.3.2 state names)"
| Segment            | Client state          | Server state              |
|--------------------|-----------------------|---------------------------|
| start              | CLOSED                | LISTEN                    |
| 1: SYN →           | SYN-SENT              | SYN-RECEIVED on receipt   |
| 2: SYN,ACK ←       | ESTABLISHED on receipt| SYN-RECEIVED              |
| 3: ACK →           | ESTABLISHED           | ESTABLISHED on receipt    |
